00:00:03.337 --> 00:00:04.920
BALA KASIVISWANATHAN:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.120
Good afternoon.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.160
My name is Bala.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.240
We're going to talk about
Apigee and the product roadmap,

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.090
but before I get
into that, I just

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.798
want to give you a
sense in terms of what

00:00:14.798 --> 00:00:16.110
we will be covering today.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.500
First and foremost, we'll
go through a section,

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.750
we'll talk about how
Apigee helps customers.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.440
Then, since we are a recent
acquisition, and some of you

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.020
are going to be Apigee
customers, some of you

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.690
GCB customers, we'll
talk about the synergies

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:33.020
of Apigee and Google Cloud.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.270
And then we'll get
into the actual details

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:38.740
of some of the roadmap
themes and focus areas.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:42.850
So before we get into the
actual roadmap presentation,

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.470
the first thing I want
to start with is APIs.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.960
We, as a company,
Apigee existed before we

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.380
got acquired by Google for
the sole purpose of helping

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:59.180
customers deliver great
APIs to their developers

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:02.150
and manage them in a good way.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.440
So why is that important?

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.200
If you think about it,
APIs are everywhere.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.490
So the way I think about this is
that if the code is the brain,

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.860
and all the technical
infrastructure we see

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:19.420
is the muscle, APIs
are kind of the air

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.380
that technology breathes, right?

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.360
It's everywhere.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.640
And we have dealt with
a lot of customers

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.490
who have a significant
volume of APIs,

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:30.710
and how we help manage that.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:36.110
But when we joined Google,
we almost saw a factor of 10

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.870
of what that happens
within Google,

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:44.030
and it gave us a lot more
excitement and capabilities

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:47.420
for thinking about, what else
could we do for our customers.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.840
So that's the context in
terms of why Apigee exists

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.540
and what's the kind of
problem we are trying

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.010
to solve for customers,
in this world where

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.550
APIs are everywhere.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.920
Now, before we get into
the actual roadmap,

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:05.010
so lets start with, where does
Apigee actually help customers?

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.700
Now, again, we're going to
talk about some roadmap items,

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:11.890
and I'm obliged to tell you
that these are our best plans.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:13.790
That are no hard
time commitments here

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:14.930
which we can adhere to.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:17.360
We'll do our best to deliver
some of these features

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:18.830
through 2017.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:21.900
So take them all with that
context as we go through.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.090
So first things first, we
see a lot of customers,

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.970
as you heard in the
keynote from Eric Schmidt,

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:32.300
that people are
currently in a place,

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.470
and they need to go to cloud.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.740
And as they're
trying to do that,

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:39.860
they go through what at least
a lot of analysts and we

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.720
call as two-speed IT.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.380
There are systems that exist
today in the enterprise, some

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.290
on premises, some in
the cloud, and they

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:50.330
are functioning
very well, and there

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.020
are some challenges with them.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.680
But what's happening is,
on the consumer side,

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.920
you already have customers
who want to go faster.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.420
They want new
mobile experiences.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.220
They want better
retail experiences.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.040
They want a better
web experience.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.975
They want better partner
integrations with respect

00:03:05.975 --> 00:03:07.710
to your businesses.

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:11.000
And when you want
to deliver those,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.770
then you get into
a lot of problems,

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:17.750
because you deal with a lot of
issues with the APIs you have.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:21.020
And this could be the
lack of visibility.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.960
Most importantly, as you
heard in the keynotes,

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.420
security becomes a big thing.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:28.730
The speed at which you can
deliver these things, that

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:30.550
becomes a big issue.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:33.590
Then when it comes to developers
who are really trying to use

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:36.150
your API-- so that's internal
developers or external

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.370
developers--

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:38.990
the challenge
becomes the ability

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:42.140
to onboard them and have
them self-serve themselves

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.750
to understanding what
the APIs and what they

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:48.110
can build using those APIs.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:49.730
The last, but not
the least, is what

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:56.210
I love to call a
successful catastrophe.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.310
What I mean by that
is that you did not

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:00.470
expect things to be popular.

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.510
And you heard the
example of Pokemon.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:04.880
So that's a good
example of what happens

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.850
when somebody plans, they
do their best estimates,

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.790
and suddenly, things go
through the roof in a good way,

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:12.890
and then you need to
deal with that, right?

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:16.910
So all those traits
apply very much to APIs.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:19.220
So when you think about
all these problems,

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.769
whether its visibility,
whether its agility,

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:24.290
whether it's security,
or the fact that you have

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:29.810
a happy coincidence of fantastic
success, and you need to scale,

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:35.060
all these problems are where
we want to help our customers.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:38.010
So where do we actually fit
in the enterprise stack,

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:38.660
so to speak?

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:40.400
Because a lot of
you have a notion

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.510
of what is in your
organizations,

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:46.380
what are you doing with
different systems today.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.700
So we are essentially
the layer which we want

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.800
to call system of engagement.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.120
And this essentially gives
you a very intelligent way

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:00.140
to manage these APIs and address
those problems we talked about,

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.540
which is, how do you
help secure your APIs,

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:05.180
how do you scale your APIs,
how do you have analytics

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.880
around your APIs,
and how do you do

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:10.206
developer management, and
last but not the least,

00:05:10.206 --> 00:05:12.830
how do you have some persistent
layer in case you want to build

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:15.650
an application quickly and take
advantage of the facilities

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:17.250
we provide you?

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.860
So this is the
context in which we

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:23.670
are able to help our customers.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.690
Now, if you take it
one more level deeper,

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:29.800
in terms of what exactly
does the product do,

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.790
we begin with the notion
that we have a developer,

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:36.484
and that's the developer
we are trying to sow.

00:05:36.484 --> 00:05:38.650
And this is something we
call a digital value chain.

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:42.070
We've been talking to customers
about this for the last five

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:44.770
years, and it resonates
very, very well,

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:47.050
because at the end of
the day, the consumer who

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:51.700
is using an app does not know,
does not care, and does not

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.434
need to know about APIs.

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:55.600
When they want to click a
button on your application

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.940
on your mobile phone, or click
a button on your website,

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.130
it should work,
it should perform,

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:02.420
and that's all they care about.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:06.860
The developer who is really
creating this application also

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:10.070
does not care what your
back-end systems are--

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:13.790
how old they are, how new
they are, are they scalable,

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:16.880
are they reliable,
are they redundant?

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:18.890
They shouldn't care
about all of that stuff.

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:20.780
They have an API,
and they are going

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:23.900
to create an application
using that API,

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.115
and it should perform
what that API says.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:33.090
The contract between the
API team and the developer

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:36.360
is the API, and
that's very important,

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:40.140
because the developer does
not need to know the team--

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.610
does not need to
know the nuances

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:44.580
of the system behind it,
which is power the API.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.650
All they care about
is the contract.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:48.780
If you tell me this is
the version 1 of API,

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:51.510
and this is what it's supposed
to do, and it's documented,

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.430
I expect it to
behave that way, I

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:56.580
expect it to perform that
way, I expect a certain SLA

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.230
for that API.

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:01.050
And as long as you do
that, my application

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.060
is going to perform
beautifully and deliver

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.160
the right experience
for my customers, which

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.490
could be your customers.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:10.550
The API team, on the other
side, is also thinking about,

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:16.100
how do I do all this when
I have a variety of systems

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.450
in my infrastructure?

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:20.480
Some of them are really modern,
containerized applications--

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:23.060
they can scale beautifully,
They are elastic--

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:26.420
whereas some of the systems are
fairly brittle, they're old,

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:29.000
but they function for
a specific reason.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:32.630
You need to figure out how
to kind of abstract all that

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:37.280
and provide a clean API for
the consumer of that API.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:39.830
So that's effectively the story
this digital value chain is

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:43.730
trying to tell you, is that
the contract is the API,

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:46.670
and the people you really
care about are the developers.

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:49.010
Again, this could be
internal developers who

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:52.370
don't need to talk to teams,
or know who is actually

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:55.190
managing the API or
managing the system,

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:57.980
or external developers
or partners who

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:00.200
could be using that API to
deliver some great value

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:03.060
for you and your customers.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:06.510
The API team is also
thinking about this as a way

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:10.440
to develop APIs, or finesse or
enrich APIs which you already

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:11.580
might have.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:13.740
And they are also
developers in our eyes,

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:17.160
and our product is also
supposed to help them and solve

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.700
the problems for them.

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:20.850
So those are the
two constituents

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.040
we try to solve for.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.470
And before I get into
any more detail on this,

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:28.260
just a show of hands, how
many of you here have heard,

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:32.175
used, or are using
Apigee right now?

00:08:32.175 --> 00:08:34.799
OK, about a 1/3 crowd here, OK.

00:08:34.799 --> 00:08:38.460
So now, let me get into
what exactly is the product.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:41.250
The product we have
is called Apigee Edge.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:43.169
And the way you want
to think about this

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:47.490
is a very important runtime
aspect of the product, which

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:50.040
is the bottom half of
the slide, and there,

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:52.080
we care a lot
about how do you do

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.650
the right security,
right transformation,

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:58.470
the right mediation for whatever
you need to make your APIs

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.300
work extremely well.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:02.340
That's job number one.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:05.430
Along with that, we
have a set of services

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:08.990
which really make life
easy for the developer who

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:11.970
is using the API, and
also the team which

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:13.860
is producing the API.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:17.070
And they include things like
delivering an awesome analytics

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:19.590
experience, so that you
can look at all the data

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:22.890
and make decisions around,
is my API performing well?

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:24.990
Does the API have the right SLA?

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.240
And is it being accessed
by different teams

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:28.800
in different parts of the world?

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.540
And all those kind
of data points

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:33.040
can be easily
visualized and seen

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:34.920
in the analytics dashboard.

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:38.430
It also allows you to very
easily publish documentation

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.890
on a developer portal and
let your developers come

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:45.420
to the portal and say, I want
to learn more about APIs.

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:48.570
It could be a store's
API, if you're a retailer,

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:51.720
or it could be a user
information, or a customer

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:53.760
information, or
account information

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:55.800
if you're in, say,
financial services.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:57.900
So I come to the
developer portal,

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.390
and I want to learn
about a certain API,

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:03.090
so I should be able to
go, search for that API,

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:04.830
look at the documentation,
and understand

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:08.310
what exactly does the API do,
and more importantly, what

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:11.070
are the steps I need
to take to consume

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:15.810
that API in a secure and
authenticated fashion?

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:17.940
And there, again, you
want the developer

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:22.170
to be very empowered to go
there and learn about that API

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:23.430
themselves.

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:26.580
And then he or she
can go and figure out

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:27.780
what they want to build.

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:29.155
What are the new
experiences they

00:10:29.155 --> 00:10:30.900
can think about or imagine?

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:35.670
And then ask for permission and
get some access to that API.

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:38.610
You want to make this process
as frictionless as possible,

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:42.370
and that's the goal of providing
your tool set like this.

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:46.510
Number three is, we've seen
many customers have large

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:48.640
set of different
organizational boundaries

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:50.650
within their company, right?

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:52.960
You have a department
A, department B,

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:55.390
and sometimes they don't
talk to each other.

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:59.230
And this is one way to make them
talk to each other in a very

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:01.760
simple, technological way.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:03.790
And when you do
that, you always also

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:06.100
have to think about all
the other obligations

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:07.720
the organization has.

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:09.737
Sometimes they could be
a regulated industry,

00:11:09.737 --> 00:11:12.070
and I've talked to a lot of
financial services customer,

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:16.360
and they go that my credit card
team cannot talk to my banking

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:20.680
team, or my banking team
cannot talk to this other team.

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:22.330
When they say it,
what they mean is,

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:25.780
the data has to be isolated, and
people who are working on those

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:28.290
APIs, and the data
behind those APIs,

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:31.210
have to be very well protected
in terms of who accesses it

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:33.277
and who cannot access it, right?

00:11:33.277 --> 00:11:35.110
So those are the kind
of things we can again

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:37.020
do with management
services, where

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:39.850
you can have a logical
separation within the product

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:40.660
we have.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:44.707
So you can create teams
which operate separately.

00:11:44.707 --> 00:11:46.540
But the beauty of this
is that you can still

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:49.930
expose all of that API
into the developer portal

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:52.630
as one consistent
kind of interface.

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:55.030
What I mean by that is,
if you're a company which

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:58.360
has developer.mycompany.com,
and if I'm a developer who

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:00.490
wants to come and
explore the APIs,

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:03.610
I actually do not care that
you have 15 departments

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:05.139
in your organization.

00:12:05.139 --> 00:12:07.180
I just want to know, what
are the things you have

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:11.440
available for me as APIs, where
we could collaborate and create

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:14.710
something interesting for our
customers, which can benefit me

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:17.230
as a business and
you as a business?

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:18.620
That's what I'm after.

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:21.940
So showing your organizational
boundaries on a developer

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:24.557
portal doesn't serve
any good, but it's

00:12:24.557 --> 00:12:26.140
something very
important when it comes

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:28.707
to providing the APIs
within your organization,

