00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:48.220
that ecosystem for yourself.

00:22:48.220 --> 00:22:50.680
And it also leads to a lot
of new business models.

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:53.490
So for example, one of the
favorite examples we have

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:56.580
as Apigee, as a
customer, Apigee customer

00:22:56.580 --> 00:22:59.120
is, for example, Walgreens.

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:00.720
Walgreens, for those--

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:02.940
I apologize, if people
have traveled here

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:06.270
internationally-- is a
local pharmacy chain,

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:10.080
and a large
brick-and-mortar chain.

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:14.340
They have always had the ability
for you to upload a picture,

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:15.970
and then print it,
and go pick it up

00:23:15.970 --> 00:23:18.890
in a nearby Walgreen's store.

00:23:18.890 --> 00:23:19.940
Now, think about that.

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:23.840
As the entire experience
shifted for people

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:26.720
who stopped taking pictures
on a digital camera,

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:29.090
and they started taking
pictures on the phone,

00:23:29.090 --> 00:23:30.740
that business
infrastructure still

00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:33.350
is there but is not
being realized in terms

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:35.310
of a new business model.

00:23:35.310 --> 00:23:37.010
So what did we do with them?

00:23:37.010 --> 00:23:40.160
So we started with them, where
we experimented with something

00:23:40.160 --> 00:23:42.260
called a photo print API.

00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:44.570
So all it does is, it
allows you to upload

00:23:44.570 --> 00:23:47.150
a picture from your phone
and then fire a print-out,

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:50.570
and figure a store near
you and pick it up, right?

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:52.400
Sounds simple enough,
and the good thing

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:54.900
is, they had already some of
the back-end systems available,

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:58.490
so this was possible, using
the technology we have

00:23:58.490 --> 00:24:00.530
and the systems they had.

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:01.640
But that's the first part.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:04.790
So you could almost say that
we took a web-based approach

00:24:04.790 --> 00:24:08.090
with digital cameras and turned
it into an API-based approach,

00:24:08.090 --> 00:24:13.160
for a mobile app plus the store,
thanks to this technology.

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:15.390
That's the starting point.

00:24:15.390 --> 00:24:18.340
Once they got that going, the
second piece of the information

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:22.470
there was, If I polled
this room, how many of you

00:24:22.470 --> 00:24:25.170
would take a picture
on your phone

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:28.400
with the native camera app?

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:31.545
OK, so this is a good
example of saying,

00:24:31.545 --> 00:24:33.170
nobody is going to
download a Walgreens

00:24:33.170 --> 00:24:35.030
app to take a picture, right?

00:24:35.030 --> 00:24:36.600
So therein lies the problem.

00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:38.270
So you're going
to take a picture

00:24:38.270 --> 00:24:40.910
with a photo-taking
app of whatever you're

00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:42.700
comfortable with.

00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:45.880
And then that's what the
next step was with Walgreens.

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:49.150
They said, OK, how do I take
this and integrate it with

00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:52.250
other applications, which are
photo-taking applications,

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:54.905
but if I let them
print onto my site--

00:24:54.905 --> 00:24:59.740
sorry, my Walgreen's store,
that would be awesome.

00:24:59.740 --> 00:25:01.630
So you, as a consumer,
get to take a picture

00:25:01.630 --> 00:25:03.077
on whatever app
you want, but you

00:25:03.077 --> 00:25:04.660
can fire a print-out
whenever you want

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:06.626
and pick it up in
a Walgreen's store.

00:25:06.626 --> 00:25:09.370
Now, that's great, right?

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:11.500
But why should a
developer do that?

00:25:11.500 --> 00:25:14.590
Guess what-- there are hundreds
of photo applications today

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:16.690
on iOS and Android,
and everybody's

00:25:16.690 --> 00:25:19.060
trying to figure out
how to monetize that.

00:25:19.060 --> 00:25:20.590
What did Walgreen's do?

00:25:20.590 --> 00:25:21.490
Said, you know what?

00:25:21.490 --> 00:25:25.010
You send a person to my
store to pick up a print-out,

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:27.230
we'll do a dev share.

00:25:27.230 --> 00:25:28.924
You send me traffic,
foot traffic,

00:25:28.924 --> 00:25:31.340
and I'll give you something
back out of the photo printing

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:32.360
revenue.

00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:33.470
Great business model.

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:36.260
That's a new business
model, right?

00:25:36.260 --> 00:25:39.020
And now you've innovated,
because you have hundreds

00:25:39.020 --> 00:25:41.970
of these app developers who have
photo-taking apps, some large,

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:44.420
some small, some very
local in certain countries,

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:46.430
in specific regions
of the country--

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:47.540
sorry, in the world--

00:25:47.540 --> 00:25:49.640
they all are trying to
do interesting things

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:51.520
with the photo-taking apps.

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:55.100
Walgreen's is not in the
photo-taking app business,

00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:56.660
so they wanted to
see how best they

00:25:56.660 --> 00:25:59.420
can take advantage of that, and
turn that into a new business

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:01.920
model, right?

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:03.600
And here's the other statistic.

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:06.300
When you have a customer
walk in to pick up

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:10.700
the printout of the
photograph, guess what happens.

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:12.960
They pick up something
else at the store.

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:15.899
So in retail, for somebody
to walk into the store,

00:26:15.899 --> 00:26:17.940
and trying to get somebody
to come into the store

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:20.270
is hard enough, but
here, you have a customer

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:23.370
who can voluntarily come
inside, pick up the print-out,

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:26.040
and they take something back
and buy something in addition

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:27.420
to what they came for.

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:29.610
That is gold in retail, right?

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:32.710
So that's the innovation
in terms of business model.

00:26:32.710 --> 00:26:34.590
But that's not where we stop.

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:36.960
Then, Walgreen's
started to think about,

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:41.880
what are the other things I do,
which can be done like this?

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:44.400
One of the things
they do is a pharmacy.

00:26:44.400 --> 00:26:47.070
Guess what's the pain point
in pharmacy-- and especially,

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:49.560
if people who are in
the US who know this--

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:53.070
is when you have to go and
fill up your prescription again

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:56.740
every month, or every 30 days,
or 15 days, or whatever it is,

00:26:56.740 --> 00:26:57.610
right?

00:26:57.610 --> 00:27:00.760
They said, if I could automate
this process, where you could

00:27:00.760 --> 00:27:03.620
take a picture of the
medicine you have,

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:07.060
and the app figures out that
you're qualified for a refill,

00:27:07.060 --> 00:27:09.775
and you're ready for a
refill, and I'll notify you

00:27:09.775 --> 00:27:12.400
when the refill is ready, so you
can pick it up on the way home

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:15.940
or on the way to the
office, life is easy, right?

00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:19.270
So they could do
that, and now they'll

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:23.027
do a refill almost
every minute, right?

00:27:23.027 --> 00:27:24.610
So that's, again,
an innovation, which

00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:27.820
it leads to brand
loyalty, great experience,

00:27:27.820 --> 00:27:30.910
and driven by an API technology.

00:27:30.910 --> 00:27:32.790
The last, but not the
least, thanks to a lot

00:27:32.790 --> 00:27:35.670
of this innovation is, now
they have a fantastic ecosystem

00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:39.480
of partners who really
are able to deliver

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:42.300
different experiences and
different integrations.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:44.910
So now you could go
to a Walgreens app

00:27:44.910 --> 00:27:47.310
and ask a question
about a condition,

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:51.192
or research a medical issue,
and then figure out an answer.

00:27:51.192 --> 00:27:52.650
And if you have
the medicine there,

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:54.960
you can see if there's
a Walgreens outlet which

00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:56.895
can actually be
nearby you, where you

00:27:56.895 --> 00:27:58.530
can go pick up that medicine.

00:27:58.530 --> 00:28:00.180
So all sorts of
innovations suddenly

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:03.700
start to happen
with that journey.

00:28:03.700 --> 00:28:06.450
So when I talk
about APIs, it's not

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:10.110
about taking your legacy
integration and exposure

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:12.600
and then just letting it be
through another technology

00:28:12.600 --> 00:28:13.350
layer.

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:16.590
But this is really
optimized for consumption.

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:19.860
And the developers can be
your own internal developers,

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:22.260
they can be external
developers or partners.

00:28:22.260 --> 00:28:24.450
But the impact you're
trying to drive

00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:27.990
is all about, how do you
drive new business models

00:28:27.990 --> 00:28:29.610
and keep innovating?

00:28:29.610 --> 00:28:31.650
And this could mean
API monetization,

00:28:31.650 --> 00:28:33.361
it could mean
partner ecosystems,

00:28:33.361 --> 00:28:35.610
it could mean different
things for different customers

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:38.940
and businesses, but
that's the actual benefit

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:41.130
that you're trying to drive.

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:43.650
So I hope that's given
you a sense, in terms

00:28:43.650 --> 00:28:47.370
of where we are trying to do
the whole ecosystem and business

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:49.470
model with a connected
business platform.

00:28:49.470 --> 00:28:51.120
And this is, again--

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:52.680
this is not new
to Google, right?

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:55.500
So today, we have over
3 million businesses

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:58.080
that use our Google Maps APIs.

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:00.150
So we know a thing
or two about what it

00:29:00.150 --> 00:29:02.040
means to have an awesome API.

00:29:02.040 --> 00:29:05.130
How do you manage this at scale,
how do you build an ecosystem,

00:29:05.130 --> 00:29:08.550
and how do you have
businesses consume those APIs

00:29:08.550 --> 00:29:10.530
for interesting things, right?

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:11.566
That's something we do.

00:29:11.566 --> 00:29:13.440
We're doing the same
thing with other things,

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:17.410
like Language API, or Vision
API, and things like that.

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:22.770
So our goal is to apply both
the learnings and the knowledge

00:29:22.770 --> 00:29:26.700
we have at Google Cloud
and Google for running

00:29:26.700 --> 00:29:30.930
scalable global APIs
and creating ecosystems,

00:29:30.930 --> 00:29:34.770
and marrying that with the
technology we have with Apigee,

00:29:34.770 --> 00:29:38.100
and also having
Orbitera there, which

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:40.800
is all about monetizing
and creating marketplace

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:43.470
for applications, all
together, to bring

00:29:43.470 --> 00:29:46.560
the value to you in a
connected business platform.

00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:48.210
So that's what we
are trying to do,

00:29:48.210 --> 00:29:51.660
and that's the synergy we
bring with Apigee, Orbitera,

00:29:51.660 --> 00:29:54.510
and Google Cloud.

00:29:54.510 --> 00:29:59.580
So, with that context, I hope
you found it really useful,

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:01.740
let's switch gears and
talk about what exactly

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:08.020
is the Apigee roadmap, and some
of the themes and focus areas.

00:30:08.020 --> 00:30:11.500
So there are three big
areas which we focus on.

00:30:11.500 --> 00:30:15.370
Number one multi-cloud,
number two,

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:18.910
delivering awesome
APIs, and number three,

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:19.900
machine intelligence.

00:30:19.900 --> 00:30:23.830
So we'll go through
each one of these.

00:30:23.830 --> 00:30:26.550
Why is multi-cloud important?

00:30:26.550 --> 00:30:31.180
We have talked to
a lot of customers.

00:30:31.180 --> 00:30:35.350
At Apigee, even before we joined
Google, I would meet at least 5

00:30:35.350 --> 00:30:37.000
to 10 customers a
week, and we have

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:40.660
a lot of other folks at Apigee
who meet customers daily.

00:30:40.660 --> 00:30:44.470
The reality of the world is
a multi-cloud world, right?

00:30:44.470 --> 00:30:47.440
People have investments
in different clouds--

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:50.110
sometimes its public clouds and
sometimes its private cloud,

00:30:50.110 --> 00:30:52.130
or on-premises data centers--

00:30:52.130 --> 00:30:55.960
but the reality, is people have
investments across the board.

00:30:55.960 --> 00:30:59.680
And it's our job,
as a product company

00:30:59.680 --> 00:31:04.570
who are helping customers take
that leap into the cloud world

00:31:04.570 --> 00:31:07.930
and make the digital
transformation, to provide you

00:31:07.930 --> 00:31:10.090
with the right tools
that's whereever you are.

00:31:10.090 --> 00:31:12.790
So today, we see customers
not only using Google Cloud,

00:31:12.790 --> 00:31:15.460
but we have customers who are
on premises using Pivotal Cloud

00:31:15.460 --> 00:31:18.100
Foundry, for example, and
using other public cloud

00:31:18.100 --> 00:31:19.870
technologies.

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:24.110
We also see people using
just their own data centers,

00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:29.080
VMs, using containers, using
Kubernetes on premises--

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:31.280
all that stuff is
happening today.

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:35.710
So we have to really help
customers, whatever they are,

00:31:35.710 --> 00:31:38.680
and that's an important,
and a very urgent need,

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:40.360
so that they can
move forward when it

00:31:40.360 --> 00:31:42.509
comes to their digital journey.

00:31:42.509 --> 00:31:44.300
So what are we doing
specifically for that?

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:46.310
So I showed you
the slide earlier,

00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:49.209
where it was really about
how our product functions.

00:31:49.209 --> 00:31:50.750
There's one difference
in this slide,

00:31:50.750 --> 00:31:53.870
which is, we have
embarked on a very

00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:56.510
aggressive hybrid approach.

00:31:56.510 --> 00:31:57.840
What does that mean?

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:01.190
That means that our
runtime layer is something,

00:32:01.190 --> 00:32:03.590
we're going to make sure
that it's available to you,

00:32:03.590 --> 00:32:07.430
and you should be able to run
it wherever you want that to be.

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:11.600
So it could be in another public
cloud, it could be on premises,

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:13.840
it could be in Pivotal
Cloud Foundry--

00:32:13.840 --> 00:32:16.130
anywhere you want
to run the runtime,

00:32:16.130 --> 00:32:17.870
you should be able to run.

00:32:17.870 --> 00:32:21.050
So in the future,
we envision a world

00:32:21.050 --> 00:32:24.260
where the runtime could be,
for example, Google Endpoints.

00:32:24.260 --> 00:32:28.520
You could have our own
gateway enterprise--

00:32:28.520 --> 00:32:31.280
Apigee hybrid
gateway, or it could

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:33.790
be even some other open-source
gateway that you're running,

00:32:33.790 --> 00:32:35.270
wherever you're running.

00:32:35.270 --> 00:32:36.860
Our job is to be
able to give you

00:32:36.860 --> 00:32:40.520
a clean, single pane of
management and control

00:32:40.520 --> 00:32:44.300
across all of that, and give you
all the other abilities we just

00:32:44.300 --> 00:32:47.150
talked about earlier
today, right?

00:32:47.150 --> 00:32:49.160
So that's the number-one goal.

00:32:49.160 --> 00:32:52.610
And I'm very happy to
announce that we actually

00:32:52.610 --> 00:32:57.030
are delivering the first part
of this, in fact, in a session

00:32:57.030 --> 00:32:58.970
one of my colleagues
is doing right now,

00:32:58.970 --> 00:33:02.000
where we're announcing the early
access program for our Edge

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:04.100
Hybrid Gateway today.

00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:09.200
And you can read about it more
on apigee.com later this week,

00:33:09.200 --> 00:33:11.300
and we'll be opening
it up for customers

00:33:11.300 --> 00:33:13.280
to sign up and start using it.

00:33:13.280 --> 00:33:15.860
And the goal here is
that A, you should

00:33:15.860 --> 00:33:17.720
be able to take
this hybrid gateway,

00:33:17.720 --> 00:33:19.850
run wherever you
want to run this,

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:23.720
and it's still managed
remotely, and it does not

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:26.390
have the constraint of having
to always stay connected

00:33:26.390 --> 00:33:29.330
with the management layer,
which is running in the cloud,

00:33:29.330 --> 00:33:31.500
or it can also be
customer managed.

00:33:31.500 --> 00:33:34.550
So everything you see on
the top half of the slide

00:33:34.550 --> 00:33:36.710
can be either Apigee
managed or can be

00:33:36.710 --> 00:33:39.950
managed by you as a customer.

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:41.740
The other important
point in the slide,

00:33:41.740 --> 00:33:44.240
which I want to make sure that
you understand from a roadmap

00:33:44.240 --> 00:33:46.430
perspective, is
that we also believe

00:33:46.430 --> 00:33:49.040
that giving you a gateway,
or giving you a runtime,

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:52.470
to run wherever you
want, is the beginning.

00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:55.050
Let's say you're running this
on a different public cloud

00:33:55.050 --> 00:33:56.580
or on Google Cloud.

00:33:56.580 --> 00:33:58.050
The next thing you
would want to do

00:33:58.050 --> 00:34:00.675
is make sure that the runtime is
able to take advantage of some

00:34:00.675 --> 00:34:02.705
of the services on that cloud.

00:34:02.705 --> 00:34:04.830
So that's the other piece
we're going to invest in,

00:34:04.830 --> 00:34:07.510
which is, give you the
native integrations out

00:34:07.510 --> 00:34:09.820
of this runtime,
wherever you need them.

00:34:09.820 --> 00:34:12.239
So for example, if you
had to do something

00:34:12.239 --> 00:34:14.982
like invoke a Google
function from the gateway,

00:34:14.982 --> 00:34:16.440
we should make it
very easy for you

00:34:16.440 --> 00:34:18.540
to do from our runtime layer.

00:34:18.540 --> 00:34:20.699
So we already have
a runtime layer

00:34:20.699 --> 00:34:22.560
which is very
sophisticated than anything

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:25.980
in the marketplace,
because we let you have--

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:26.928
run Node code in it.

00:34:26.928 --> 00:34:28.469
You can actually do
service call-outs

00:34:28.469 --> 00:34:31.020
using Java, Python,
or JavaScript.

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:33.022
All of that
functionality is awesome,

00:34:33.022 --> 00:34:34.980
but when you're running
that particular runtime

00:34:34.980 --> 00:34:37.440
layer in a cloud, you
should be able to access

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:40.800
all the functions of that
particular cloud very easily

00:34:40.800 --> 00:34:42.840
and should be able
to integrate that.

00:34:42.840 --> 00:34:44.670
So that's the other
important aspect

00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:47.800
of how we're thinking about
this whole hybrid approach.

00:34:47.800 --> 00:34:51.790
So again, to summarize, the key
takeaway from this slide is, A,

00:34:51.790 --> 00:34:54.210
we are committed as
Apigee to multi-cloud.

00:34:54.210 --> 00:34:57.750
Number two, our big
bet is around hybrid,

00:34:57.750 --> 00:35:02.130
so you should be able to run
our runtime layer wherever

00:35:02.130 --> 00:35:03.720
you want to run this.

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:06.060
And number three, it
should provide you

00:35:06.060 --> 00:35:08.340
all kinds of native
integration capabilities

00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:10.230
over a period of time,
so that depending

00:35:10.230 --> 00:35:12.450
on the cloud you're using,
you should take advantage

00:35:12.450 --> 00:35:14.880
of that native facility, OK?

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:16.980
That's multi-cloud.

00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:18.240
Let's look at APIs.

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:19.687
So when I think
about APIs, there

00:35:19.687 --> 00:35:22.020
are two things which we need
to really, really make sure

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:23.220
that happens.

00:35:23.220 --> 00:35:27.090
Every CIO, every
product executive,

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:32.920
every API program owner I
talk to, talks about security.

00:35:32.920 --> 00:35:37.240
It's not a cloud topic, it's
also an API platform topic.

00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:38.770
At the end of the
day, when you are

00:35:38.770 --> 00:35:44.260
trying to expose your APIs to
the world or to your partners,

00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:46.120
security is key.

00:35:46.120 --> 00:35:49.210
So we will continue to innovate
and deliver the best security

00:35:49.210 --> 00:35:51.280
features you can
have when it comes

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:53.770
to the runtime
and managing APIs.

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:56.860
So we just went live last
week with the SAML support

00:35:56.860 --> 00:35:58.330
and for our core offering.

00:35:58.330 --> 00:36:01.540
That means you should be
able to use any SAML provider

00:36:01.540 --> 00:36:04.570
for authenticating both our
management layer as well

00:36:04.570 --> 00:36:06.070
as the runtime layer.

00:36:06.070 --> 00:36:07.690
That gives you a
lot of flexibility

00:36:07.690 --> 00:36:10.590
in terms of controls, in
terms of who authenticate--

00:36:10.590 --> 00:36:13.150
who is able to access and
authenticate themselves

00:36:13.150 --> 00:36:15.390
in using APIs.

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:18.430
We are also making sure
that it's very easy for you

00:36:18.430 --> 00:36:21.400
to manage certificates when
it comes to two-way TLS

00:36:21.400 --> 00:36:23.830
connections or
anything else, again,

00:36:23.830 --> 00:36:27.070
giving you the ability to watch
for when they're expiring,

00:36:27.070 --> 00:36:31.360
renewing them, making sure the
API SLAs are not going down

00:36:31.360 --> 00:36:33.670
because of those kind of issues.

00:36:33.670 --> 00:36:36.370
There are other two big themes
which I want to hit upon here.

00:36:36.370 --> 00:36:41.950
One is, we do want to make sure
that as organizations adopt

00:36:41.950 --> 00:36:44.590
the API-first approach,
what we are seeing

00:36:44.590 --> 00:36:46.900
is a larger pool
of folks who are

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:51.200
trying to help drive the overall
digital journey in the company.

00:36:51.200 --> 00:36:53.430
And that brings in a
lot of security issue,

00:36:53.430 --> 00:36:55.900
which-- it's with
respect to people

00:36:55.900 --> 00:36:58.060
and what they can access.

00:36:58.060 --> 00:37:00.640
So we're investing
specific set of energy

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:04.320
around making sure that we
don't create a friction,

00:37:04.320 --> 00:37:06.070
because one of the
things we want to avoid

00:37:06.070 --> 00:37:10.630
is a very strong, very
top-down governance

00:37:10.630 --> 00:37:13.600
model, because what happens
with that is, it takes away

00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:16.480
the speed and the agility
and the interations

00:37:16.480 --> 00:37:18.784
you need in an API program.

00:37:18.784 --> 00:37:20.200
So instead, what
we're going to do

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:25.960
is provide you very easy ways
to either live off your existing

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:28.970
authorization models,
and create ad hoc teams

00:37:28.970 --> 00:37:31.630
and permissions
services on top of it,

00:37:31.630 --> 00:37:33.400
or give you a very
lightweight model

00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:35.230
to create the
right set of folks,

00:37:35.230 --> 00:37:37.780
and the right set of permissions
for what they need to do,

00:37:37.780 --> 00:37:39.520
on top of a platform.

00:37:39.520 --> 00:37:43.600
The essence here is to give
you visibility, control,

00:37:43.600 --> 00:37:46.020
but at speed and scale.

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:48.160
That's the goal out
here, not to give you

00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:52.150
a very stoic, straitjacketed
way to do something in terms

00:37:52.150 --> 00:37:54.550
of actual governance models.

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:56.380
Last but not the least,
we also understand

00:37:56.380 --> 00:37:58.660
that there are a lot of
existing ways in which you

00:37:58.660 --> 00:38:00.490
can manage keys.

00:38:00.490 --> 00:38:02.470
So it could be for
rotations, could

00:38:02.470 --> 00:38:04.570
be for encryption,
and things like that,

00:38:04.570 --> 00:38:06.640
and you have existing
policies, and you may

00:38:06.640 --> 00:38:08.196
have existing infrastructure.

00:38:08.196 --> 00:38:09.820
And one of the things
we're going to do

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:13.642
is make sure that we invest
enough to integrate with those

00:38:13.642 --> 00:38:15.100
so you don't have
to reinvent them,

00:38:15.100 --> 00:38:18.582
because you may have gone
through a lot of due diligence

00:38:18.582 --> 00:38:21.040
in making sure that they are
the ones which are helping you

00:38:21.040 --> 00:38:26.361
with respect to compliance, and
some sort of regulatory burden,

00:38:26.361 --> 00:38:26.860
right?

00:38:26.860 --> 00:38:29.410
So we want to make
sure that's there.

00:38:29.410 --> 00:38:30.780
So this is security.

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:34.060
So for everything
we do in security,

00:38:34.060 --> 00:38:36.090
which is to make sure
that your APIs are safe

00:38:36.090 --> 00:38:39.430
and your obligations around
compliance are there.

00:38:39.430 --> 00:38:41.280
We also want to
make sure that we

00:38:41.280 --> 00:38:44.220
have a fantastic,
delightful user of the API

00:38:44.220 --> 00:38:47.670
and the product we have so
that you can do your best

00:38:47.670 --> 00:38:50.022
work with our product.

00:38:50.022 --> 00:38:51.480
So first thing
we're doing there is

00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:54.939
that we're moving from our
classic UI, which kind of is

00:38:54.939 --> 00:38:56.730
what we've been having
for about 3 and 1/2,

00:38:56.730 --> 00:38:59.430
4 years now, to something
we just launched

00:38:59.430 --> 00:39:02.640
late last year, towards
the end of the year, which

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:04.440
is this kind of an experience.

00:39:04.440 --> 00:39:10.020
So it's not just a cleaner
modern design in terms of UI,

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:12.930
but it's very focused around
what you want to get done

00:39:12.930 --> 00:39:15.790
and how fast you want
to get things done.

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:18.820
There are a few things we want
to do very much out of this,

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:22.690
which is, number one is, we
want to make sure that we make

00:39:22.690 --> 00:39:26.590
the OpenAPI Spec a first-class
citizen within our UI

00:39:26.590 --> 00:39:28.420
and our product.

00:39:28.420 --> 00:39:31.840
And for those who are already
familiar with OpenAPI Spec,

00:39:31.840 --> 00:39:34.130
you know the importance
of this open standard,

00:39:34.130 --> 00:39:37.840
that you can describe and
have the right kind of API

00:39:37.840 --> 00:39:38.830
specifications.

00:39:38.830 --> 00:39:40.300
You can collaborate on them.

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:42.787
And so we provide a very
simple in-line [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:42.787 --> 00:39:44.620
were you can actually
collaborate and create

00:39:44.620 --> 00:39:46.420
the specifications.

00:39:46.420 --> 00:39:48.080
But we don't stop there.

00:39:48.080 --> 00:39:50.872
We let you create the
right API proxies right out

00:39:50.872 --> 00:39:52.330
of those specifications
so that you

00:39:52.330 --> 00:39:55.910
can go faster in terms of what
do you want to do with it.

00:39:55.910 --> 00:39:58.720
You can even mock an API, and
have your developer consume

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:00.490
that API, and build
an application

00:40:00.490 --> 00:40:03.040
without actually really
having a back-end system.

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:05.260
Those are the kind of things
you could do with what

00:40:05.260 --> 00:40:08.540
we have in our product.

00:40:08.540 --> 00:40:11.890
The other thing you do is, once
you have an API proxy or API,

00:40:11.890 --> 00:40:13.330
you should be able
to think about,

00:40:13.330 --> 00:40:17.020
how do you want to package it
for the consumers of this API?

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:20.140
As I mentioned before, this is
all about the consumption, not

00:40:20.140 --> 00:40:21.520
about the exposure.

00:40:21.520 --> 00:40:22.940
So we do that as well.

00:40:22.940 --> 00:40:26.380
And that means you can package
up, create the right products,

00:40:26.380 --> 00:40:29.770
and then publish them in a
portal in a very easy way.

00:40:29.770 --> 00:40:33.010
So again, in this particular
exercise, what we have done is,

00:40:33.010 --> 00:40:36.130
we've let you do a very, very
simple, easy portal, which

00:40:36.130 --> 00:40:40.240
is configurable, which you
can go and publish everything

00:40:40.240 --> 00:40:42.520
in terms of documentation
and your products,

00:40:42.520 --> 00:40:45.940
and get your dev portal--
developer portal ready

00:40:45.940 --> 00:40:47.230
in under 10 minutes.

00:40:47.230 --> 00:40:49.452
And that's the goal we have.

00:40:49.452 --> 00:40:50.910
And last but not
the least, we want

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:53.250
to make sure that
everything else you do here

00:40:53.250 --> 00:40:56.340
is easy, self-serve, and you
are able to actually take

00:40:56.340 --> 00:40:58.920
advantage of the
steps we provide you,

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:00.580
and you can explore
the right way.

00:41:00.580 --> 00:41:03.090
So we made sure that this
particular experience

00:41:03.090 --> 00:41:05.820
is very, very intuitive, and
it's very helpful for you

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:07.640
to be more productive.

00:41:07.640 --> 00:41:10.460
So in terms of the actual focus,
in terms of future themes,

00:41:10.460 --> 00:41:13.750
we continue to do
OpenAPI support.

00:41:13.750 --> 00:41:17.770
And in fact, there is a current
draft of OpenAPI Spec 3.0,

00:41:17.770 --> 00:41:19.870
so as it gets ratified,
we want to make sure

00:41:19.870 --> 00:41:21.940
that we are on top of
that and support you

00:41:21.940 --> 00:41:24.610
in having that support.

00:41:24.610 --> 00:41:27.070
We continue to
improve and innovate

00:41:27.070 --> 00:41:29.010
on our next-gen
developer portal,

00:41:29.010 --> 00:41:31.660
and giving you more
features, and making it

00:41:31.660 --> 00:41:34.360
configurable, yet powerful.

00:41:34.360 --> 00:41:36.550
And this also means we
give you two options.

00:41:36.550 --> 00:41:39.070
We have a very
strong customer base,

00:41:39.070 --> 00:41:41.320
which uses our Drupal
Developer Portal, which

00:41:41.320 --> 00:41:45.520
is extremely flexible,
very highly customizable,

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:47.230
and takes advantage
of a large community

00:41:47.230 --> 00:41:51.091
of open-source development
around the Drupal technology.

00:41:51.091 --> 00:41:52.840
But we're also going
to give you something

00:41:52.840 --> 00:41:56.290
which is a very simple, hosted,
configurable portal, which

00:41:56.290 --> 00:41:59.290
you can go and start
up very easily.

00:41:59.290 --> 00:42:01.900
As people mature in
their API journey,

00:42:01.900 --> 00:42:03.910
we are also seeing
that a lot of folks

00:42:03.910 --> 00:42:06.370
want to actually start
thinking about monetization.

00:42:06.370 --> 00:42:08.320
So one of the things
you'll see is,

00:42:08.320 --> 00:42:10.780
while we have a fantastic
monetization product,

00:42:10.780 --> 00:42:13.630
we built it for
large customers who

00:42:13.630 --> 00:42:15.970
have complex business models.

00:42:15.970 --> 00:42:17.770
We are going to
invest in making sure

00:42:17.770 --> 00:42:20.800
that you have very easy and--

00:42:20.800 --> 00:42:23.620
easy ways to monetize the
APIs, and probably very simple

00:42:23.620 --> 00:42:27.052
ways to try what you can in the
marketplace and keep adjusting,

00:42:27.052 --> 00:42:28.510
so you can find
out what's the best

00:42:28.510 --> 00:42:30.580
way to go and create
those new business

00:42:30.580 --> 00:42:32.860
models which you want to do.

00:42:32.860 --> 00:42:35.740
Last but not the
least, any API program,

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:39.010
for it to be successful,
needs to have a good community

00:42:39.010 --> 00:42:39.760
participation.

00:42:39.760 --> 00:42:42.370
And this community could be
all your internal developers,

00:42:42.370 --> 00:42:45.220
and it could be all the
developers in your partner,

00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:46.612
or a broader ecosystem.

00:42:46.612 --> 00:42:48.820
We want to make sure that
we give you the right tools

00:42:48.820 --> 00:42:50.830
to bring in that
community, or you

00:42:50.830 --> 00:42:54.820
to get access to that community,
as part of the product itself.

00:42:54.820 --> 00:42:57.760
So that's kind of
the other aspect.

00:42:57.760 --> 00:43:04.120
So as we look at all of these,
the key takeaway for-- sorry,

00:43:04.120 --> 00:43:04.720
going back--

00:43:04.720 --> 00:43:07.480
the key takeaway for APIs
is, we want to make sure

00:43:07.480 --> 00:43:10.420
that we deliver secure APIs.

00:43:10.420 --> 00:43:13.030
And we make sure that the APIs
are available for consumption

00:43:13.030 --> 00:43:14.200
in a easy way.

00:43:14.200 --> 00:43:17.190
And that's kind of our
goal with the APIs.

00:43:17.190 --> 00:43:19.480
Let's go to the third and--

00:43:19.480 --> 00:43:23.100
interesting aspect of
machine intelligence.

00:43:23.100 --> 00:43:25.740
I'm sure you're hearing
everything from, you know,

00:43:25.740 --> 00:43:30.720
self-driving cars to
artificial intelligence.

00:43:30.720 --> 00:43:33.000
All of this stuff is exciting.

00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:34.500
It's very, very exciting.

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:38.730
And we are really hoping that
as a Google Cloud customer,

00:43:38.730 --> 00:43:41.040
you're able to take
advantage of all the big data

00:43:41.040 --> 00:43:43.290
and the machine
learning APIs we have

00:43:43.290 --> 00:43:47.070
and build something awesome,
like you heard in the keynote.

00:43:47.070 --> 00:43:51.225
While we do that, we, as
Apigee, are also looking at this

00:43:51.225 --> 00:43:55.560
and going, what is the stuff
we can do in our product, which

00:43:55.560 --> 00:43:58.710
allows us to make the
product shine better,

00:43:58.710 --> 00:44:00.420
thanks to these technologies?

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:03.079
And that's in a very
important initiative for us.

00:44:03.079 --> 00:44:05.370
So I'm going to walk you
through some examples in terms

00:44:05.370 --> 00:44:06.930
of what we're
trying to do when it

00:44:06.930 --> 00:44:09.880
comes to machine intelligence.

00:44:09.880 --> 00:44:13.620
The first thing we realized
is that once you have APIs,

00:44:13.620 --> 00:44:18.710
the scale at which APIs
can get abused or attacked

00:44:18.710 --> 00:44:22.990
is significantly different than
what you've seen before, right?

00:44:22.990 --> 00:44:26.110
Because there will be
somebody who leaks an API key,

00:44:26.110 --> 00:44:28.750
and you'll have
sophisticated programs.

00:44:28.750 --> 00:44:32.530
They will come at that API
from different IP addresses,

00:44:32.530 --> 00:44:34.630
at different times,
at different speeds,

00:44:34.630 --> 00:44:36.700
in terms of requests per second.

00:44:36.700 --> 00:44:40.550
So a simple rule-based
approach just doesn't work.

00:44:40.550 --> 00:44:42.750
So all our APIs, you
could do something like

00:44:42.750 --> 00:44:44.680
put a spike [INAUDIBLE] policy.

00:44:44.680 --> 00:44:47.440
Like, you can say, OK, more
than 1,000 requests per second?

00:44:47.440 --> 00:44:49.990
Just shut down-- don't respond.

00:44:49.990 --> 00:44:53.140
But the advantage these bots
have is, they can learn.

00:44:53.140 --> 00:44:55.910
They can go out and you at
1,000 requests per second,

00:44:55.910 --> 00:44:57.670
and once they know
you're getting blocked,

00:44:57.670 --> 00:45:01.960
they can keep coming
at you at 999, right?

00:45:01.960 --> 00:45:04.790
And then you say, no, I'll
figure out the IP addresses.

00:45:04.790 --> 00:45:07.870
But if it's a mobile client
API, but it's coming from--

00:45:07.870 --> 00:45:09.700
in a public cloud
IP address, maybe I

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:11.230
can shut it down immediately.

00:45:11.230 --> 00:45:12.710
They can spawn IP addresses.

00:45:12.710 --> 00:45:15.340
So you can keep chasing
these things, right?

00:45:15.340 --> 00:45:17.640
So the goal here
is to provide you

00:45:17.640 --> 00:45:20.680
with tools which are able to
prevent those bot attacks,

00:45:20.680 --> 00:45:24.070
and also make sure that you're
able to detect them correctly,

00:45:24.070 --> 00:45:27.520
and then make sure that you're
able to prevent those attacks

00:45:27.520 --> 00:45:29.260
from taking your systems down.

00:45:29.260 --> 00:45:30.312
And how do we do that?

00:45:30.312 --> 00:45:32.020
And this is where we
are taking advantage

00:45:32.020 --> 00:45:35.110
of the machine learning
capabilities at Google

00:45:35.110 --> 00:45:38.290
and applying them to a very
specific context here, which

00:45:38.290 --> 00:45:39.970
is the APIs, right?

00:45:39.970 --> 00:45:42.910
So here, we would look at
patterns-- patterns meaning,

00:45:42.910 --> 00:45:44.950
is there a slow-burn call?

00:45:44.950 --> 00:45:45.950
Where is it coming from?

00:45:45.950 --> 00:45:47.080
Is it from multiple places?

00:45:47.080 --> 00:45:50.470
Is it for a specific
resource on that API?

00:45:50.470 --> 00:45:51.850
What times do it come--

00:45:51.850 --> 00:45:53.110
do they come at?

00:45:53.110 --> 00:45:56.130
All these things can be done,
and learned continuously,

00:45:56.130 --> 00:45:57.880
so that we can give
you the right warning,

00:45:57.880 --> 00:46:01.745
saying, hey, these API calls
looks like bot attacks,

00:46:01.745 --> 00:46:04.120
so you need to do something,
and give you the right tools

00:46:04.120 --> 00:46:04.990
to do it.

00:46:04.990 --> 00:46:08.530
Or in some cases, we actually
know it's a bot attack,

00:46:08.530 --> 00:46:11.590
and we can do some preemptive
action on your behalf.

00:46:11.590 --> 00:46:14.070
And that's something
which can only be done

00:46:14.070 --> 00:46:17.520
at scale with machine learning.

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:19.950
There's no other way to do
this because of the volume--

00:46:19.950 --> 00:46:21.990
sheer volume and
magnitude of these kind

00:46:21.990 --> 00:46:24.420
of attacks that can happen.

00:46:24.420 --> 00:46:26.490
The second one is
about user engagement.

00:46:26.490 --> 00:46:29.880
A lot of people use APIs
for delivering experiences

00:46:29.880 --> 00:46:31.740
on mobile apps or web apps.

00:46:31.740 --> 00:46:35.610
And the good news
is that you can

00:46:35.610 --> 00:46:39.540
do some fantastic personalized
experiences to users.

00:46:39.540 --> 00:46:43.140
The bad news is, if you don't
keep learning and changing,

00:46:43.140 --> 00:46:45.502
you're going to deliver some
bad experience pretty soon,

00:46:45.502 --> 00:46:47.460
because it's not, again,
a rule-based approach.

00:46:47.460 --> 00:46:49.650
You got to look at
different factors

00:46:49.650 --> 00:46:51.810
and deliver the
right kind of value.

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:54.000
And when you can
inject something

00:46:54.000 --> 00:46:57.690
in that API to change the
experience for that customer

00:46:57.690 --> 00:47:00.630
on the mobile app, and
deliver a very specific user

00:47:00.630 --> 00:47:04.540
engagement or personalization,
it takes it to a new level.

00:47:04.540 --> 00:47:07.110
So again, that's an area where
we want to invest and give you

00:47:07.110 --> 00:47:11.070
the right tool sets, so you
can configure and attach

00:47:11.070 --> 00:47:13.290
some models so that
you can say, here's

00:47:13.290 --> 00:47:16.410
how I want the app to behave
if these are the things you

00:47:16.410 --> 00:47:19.410
notice, and have that
model learn continuously,

00:47:19.410 --> 00:47:23.050
and improve the experience
you deliver to your customers.

00:47:23.050 --> 00:47:26.530
The last one is an
example of operations.

00:47:26.530 --> 00:47:27.940
What do I mean by operations?

00:47:27.940 --> 00:47:29.680
So there are a couple
of examples, right?

00:47:29.680 --> 00:47:31.810
So you're creating
an API proxy, and you

00:47:31.810 --> 00:47:37.150
happen to create one for,
let's say, user account.

00:47:37.150 --> 00:47:39.640
So when it comes
to user account,

00:47:39.640 --> 00:47:43.690
we may have seen hundreds
of user account APIs

00:47:43.690 --> 00:47:46.150
across our customers,
and we would probably

00:47:46.150 --> 00:47:50.110
also know what are the typical
traits of a user account?

00:47:50.110 --> 00:47:52.610
What are the logging
patterns of a user account?

00:47:52.610 --> 00:47:54.040
What is the traffic like?

00:47:54.040 --> 00:47:56.080
Is it a time and date--

00:47:56.080 --> 00:47:59.860
sorry, a frequency in
which they get used?

00:47:59.860 --> 00:48:03.610
Or in terms of, do they have
to have some security policy,

00:48:03.610 --> 00:48:06.352
because people typically try
to attack that, from a bot

00:48:06.352 --> 00:48:07.810
perspective, because
they're trying

00:48:07.810 --> 00:48:10.597
to get into an account
with brute force.

00:48:10.597 --> 00:48:12.430
So there are a lot of
things we can actually

00:48:12.430 --> 00:48:15.160
glean from all the
patterns we watch,

00:48:15.160 --> 00:48:18.010
across a set of
typically known APIs--

00:48:18.010 --> 00:48:21.340
could be slash accounts,
could be slash login.

00:48:21.340 --> 00:48:23.590
How can we help
you say that when

00:48:23.590 --> 00:48:27.130
you're creating a slash login
API for the first time using

00:48:27.130 --> 00:48:28.300
our product?

00:48:28.300 --> 00:48:32.260
We say, based on all
the customers we know,

00:48:32.260 --> 00:48:33.880
we highly recommend
that you have

00:48:33.880 --> 00:48:37.830
these two policies for
security with the login API.

00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:39.610
That would be awesome.

00:48:39.610 --> 00:48:43.570
In fact, even better would
be, as we learn more,

00:48:43.570 --> 00:48:46.690
we actually prompt you, even
after the API is in production,

00:48:46.690 --> 00:48:48.700
to say, hey, we
notice your login

00:48:48.700 --> 00:48:52.060
API has been running for three
months, and blah, blah, blah.

00:48:52.060 --> 00:48:54.370
We highly recommend, based
on some of the new threats

00:48:54.370 --> 00:48:57.910
we've seen, you add this
additional policy to your API.

00:48:57.910 --> 00:49:00.490
That is something we can do
for you, both from a security

00:49:00.490 --> 00:49:03.730
perspective and making
your operational nightmare

00:49:03.730 --> 00:49:05.680
a little bit easier, right?

00:49:05.680 --> 00:49:08.530
The second one
could be rollouts.

00:49:08.530 --> 00:49:11.260
We released about
every two weeks,

00:49:11.260 --> 00:49:14.620
and we've gotten into this
blue-green model of deployments

00:49:14.620 --> 00:49:17.080
so that we know,
when all our customer

00:49:17.080 --> 00:49:18.970
API traffic is coming
in, we can start

00:49:18.970 --> 00:49:21.580
with the percentage of traffic
switched to a new release,

00:49:21.580 --> 00:49:24.460
watch it, look at the data,
look at the error rates,

00:49:24.460 --> 00:49:27.130
and keep increasing the
percentage for the new release

00:49:27.130 --> 00:49:29.650
before we switch over 100%.

00:49:29.650 --> 00:49:32.590
How would we be able to give
that kind of information

00:49:32.590 --> 00:49:35.140
to you for your own APIs?

00:49:35.140 --> 00:49:39.610
Can we look at patterns of, when
you switch from a V1 to V2 API,

00:49:39.610 --> 00:49:41.770
could we give you some
knowledge and say,

00:49:41.770 --> 00:49:44.620
looks like your error rates
are way beyond the threshold.

00:49:44.620 --> 00:49:46.990
We suggest to roll
back immediately.

00:49:46.990 --> 00:49:49.540
Or you're looking really
good, so you should accelerate

00:49:49.540 --> 00:49:52.300
the release cycle right now.

00:49:52.300 --> 00:49:54.607
What are the kind of things
we can automate for you?

00:49:54.607 --> 00:49:57.190
Is there things we can do where
we look at certain error rates

00:49:57.190 --> 00:50:00.580
and automatically tell
you or your ops person

00:50:00.580 --> 00:50:03.190
to do some changes to
your back-end systems?

00:50:03.190 --> 00:50:05.920
Those are the kind of things
we can automate, again,

00:50:05.920 --> 00:50:08.950
by learning continuously
and applying some patterns

00:50:08.950 --> 00:50:10.850
and algos to it.

00:50:10.850 --> 00:50:13.010
So again, this is
extremely useful

00:50:13.010 --> 00:50:16.900
when the volumes are at
a different scale, right?

00:50:16.900 --> 00:50:21.400
And we believe the billion
calls a day we're processing

00:50:21.400 --> 00:50:24.190
is going to be nothing
very, very soon.

00:50:24.190 --> 00:50:27.100
And the data we see
across our customers

00:50:27.100 --> 00:50:31.030
is that everybody is growing
in terms of their API calls,

00:50:31.030 --> 00:50:34.210
because everybody is trying
to take an API-first approach.

00:50:34.210 --> 00:50:36.640
When you have that
kind of a scale,

00:50:36.640 --> 00:50:38.950
there is nothing that
is going to help better

00:50:38.950 --> 00:50:41.530
than having something
like a machine

00:50:41.530 --> 00:50:45.820
learning technology which
applies in the API context.

00:50:45.820 --> 00:50:48.930
So that's kind of
the third big market.

00:50:48.930 --> 00:50:51.750
So in summary,
the three bit bets

00:50:51.750 --> 00:50:55.890
are multi-cloud, delivering
great APIs, and machine

00:50:55.890 --> 00:50:57.090
intelligence.

00:50:57.090 --> 00:50:59.220
And what the
takeaway would be is

00:50:59.220 --> 00:51:00.960
in terms of making
sure that it's

00:51:00.960 --> 00:51:04.820
an API platform that
will be available to you

00:51:04.820 --> 00:51:06.690
where you want it.

00:51:06.690 --> 00:51:10.740
We want to deliver you the most
secure and easy-to-use APIs

00:51:10.740 --> 00:51:12.270
using our technology.

00:51:12.270 --> 00:51:16.770
And number three, we want to
present to you, in our product,

00:51:16.770 --> 00:51:19.860
intelligence as an
ingredient so that you

00:51:19.860 --> 00:51:21.330
don't have to do the hard work.

00:51:21.330 --> 00:51:22.800
We do everything
behind the scenes,

00:51:22.800 --> 00:51:25.200
thanks to all the
power of Google Cloud,

00:51:25.200 --> 00:51:29.170
and deliver intelligent
features for you in the product.

00:51:29.170 --> 00:51:32.970
So those are the three
big themes we have.

00:51:32.970 --> 00:51:37.320
And we are very, very excited
to be part of Google Cloud

00:51:37.320 --> 00:51:39.150
and Google, and
also look forward

00:51:39.150 --> 00:51:41.550
to delivering some of
these amazing features.

00:51:41.550 --> 00:51:43.634
And hopefully, some of
you who are our customers

00:51:43.634 --> 00:51:46.050
will be taking advantage of
this through our early adopter

00:51:46.050 --> 00:51:47.010
program.

00:51:47.010 --> 00:51:48.790
And those who are
not yet customers,

00:51:48.790 --> 00:51:53.040
I hope we have your trust,
and we'll get you as customers

00:51:53.040 --> 00:51:55.350
and get into a
great partnership.

00:51:55.350 --> 00:51:58.860
So with that, I want to
thank you for taking the time

00:51:58.860 --> 00:52:00.234
and attending this session.

00:52:00.234 --> 00:52:01.650
And I'll be around
in case anybody

00:52:01.650 --> 00:52:03.330
wants any questions answered.

00:52:03.330 --> 00:52:05.004
I'll be more than
happy to take them.

00:52:05.004 --> 00:52:05.670
Thank you again.

